1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,720
Your goal is simple: eliminate all disease. The constraint is also simple: resources are scarce.

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:14,160
You have limited money, time, and talent. Every dollar on a low-impact trial is a dollar not curing cancer.

3
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:19,560
Every researcher writing grant applications is a researcher not doing research.

4
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:25,720
Every patient excluded from trials is data you'll never collect and a person you'll never help.

5
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:31,460
Your current system fails this optimization so thoroughly it almost looks intentional.

6
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:38,040
Your National Institutes of Health, or N.I.H., spends forty-seven billion dollars a year.

7
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:44,660
Your scientists spend 50 to 67 percent of their time writing grant applications instead of doing science.

8
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:51,200
Billions flow to projects that never produce treatments. Your system isn't designed to maximize cures.

9
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:55,520
It's designed to maximize grant-writing. This isn't a conspiracy.

10
00:00:55,820 --> 00:01:01,420
It's just what happens when you design a system that rewards asking for money instead of producing results.

11
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:10,160
You've accidentally built a machine whose primary output is paperwork and whose secondary output is occasionally, reluctantly,

12
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:17,460
medicine. Your Decentralized Institute of Health, or D.I.H., is an alternative design that optimizes for a single metric:

13
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,940
maximum return on investment, or R.O.I., toward disease eradication.

14
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:27,060
It's what you'd build if you started from scratch and actually wanted results.

15
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:31,600
The Health-Industrial Complex: Coordinating Your War on Disease.

16
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:40,600
The Olsonian Problem. Your economist Mancur Olson identified why public goods are systematically underproduced: diffuse

17
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:48,340
benefits and concentrated costs. In simpler terms: everyone benefits from a cure for cancer, but nobody benefits enough

18
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:52,220
to fight for it the way a weapons manufacturer fights for a bomber contract.

19
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:59,400
It's one of those observations that's obvious once someone says it, and invisible before, which describes most of your species'

20
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:04,300
problems. Curing cancer benefits 8 billion people a little.

21
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:09,759
Blocking cancer cures benefits a few thousand pharmaceutical executives a lot.

22
00:02:10,419 --> 00:02:18,680
The executives show up to lobby. The 8 billion don't. This is why your species has spent 50 years "fighting cancer" while

23
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:22,940
your defense industry got stealth bombers, aircraft carriers, and G.P.S.

24
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:27,340
Your military-industrial complex solved this problem for defense.

25
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:34,660
Defense contractors, generals, politicians, and workers all have concentrated interests in military spending.

26
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:41,600
They coordinate. They lobby. They win budgets. Result: the most powerful military in human history.

27
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:47,840
Disease has no such coalition. Your patients are too sick to lobby.

28
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:52,180
Your researchers compete for scraps. Your funders lack coordination.

29
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:58,860
Your politicians get no credit for cures that arrive after their term ends (which is all of them, because cures take 15

30
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:06,880
years and terms last 4). Everyone wants disease eradicated; no one has a concentrated interest in making it happen.

31
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:14,140
It's like everyone wanting a clean kitchen but nobody wanting to do the dishes, except the dishes are cancer and the kitchen

32
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:23,030
is on fire. Your D.I.H. solves the Olsonian problem by creating concentrated interests in disease eradication.

33
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:30,940
See The Incentive Stack for how. Your military-industrial complex coordinates actors around defense.

34
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:37,240
Your D.I.H. creates a health-industrial complex that coordinates actors around eradication.

35
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:42,560
Same structure, opposite purpose. SHAEF for Your War on Disease.

36
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:49,180
In 1944, Eisenhower didn't replace the Allied armies. He coordinated them.

37
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:55,040
Set the objective, allocated resources, made sure everyone pulled in the same direction.

38
00:03:56,120 --> 00:04:03,140
Your D.I.H. is SHAEF for the war on disease. Pharma companies stay pharma companies.

39
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:08,500
Universities stay universities. Patient groups stay patient groups.

40
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:14,340
But they operate as one force because your D.I.H. coordinates them toward eradication.

41
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:20,820
Your war on disease has been losing for 50 years because it's not actually a war.

42
00:04:21,339 --> 00:04:29,180
It's a collection of uncoordinated skirmishes where researchers compete for grants, pharma companies hide failures, and

43
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:39,100
patients can't access trials. You've been "fighting" cancer the way a cat "fights" a laser pointer: lots of energy, no coordination,

44
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:46,720
nothing caught. Imagine if D-Day had been run this way. The Americans land at one beach, the British at another.

45
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:54,230
Neither tells the other what they learned. Both compete for the same supply ships while the enemy reads their grant applications.

46
00:04:54,840 --> 00:05:02,240
Your D.I.H. is the coordination layer that turns scattered skirmishes into a unified campaign.

47
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:08,160
Your D.I.H.: The return on investment, or R.O.I., Maximization Protocol.

48
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:14,800
Your D.I.H. is not a platform. It's not an organization. It's not even really a thing.

49
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:21,550
It's a coordination protocol with one function: "Make every actor do the highest-R.O.I.

50
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:27,160
thing toward total disease eradication." Every dollar flows to maximum impact.

51
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:33,600
Every researcher works on highest-value problems. Every patient joins trials that matter most.

52
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:40,560
Nothing gets wasted on low-R.O.I. activities. On your planet, you call this radical.

53
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:49,720
On mine, we call it "obvious.". Three Core Functions. Receive funds (from the one percent Treaty, donations, etc.).

54
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:56,710
Allocate research via patient subsidies (market mechanism) and infrastructure via Wishocracy.

55
00:05:57,260 --> 00:06:02,400
Verify results and pay proportional to impact. Everything operational is outsourced.

56
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:08,160
Trial infrastructure? That's your decentralized F.D.A.'s, or D.F.D.A.'s, job.

57
00:06:09,080 --> 00:06:14,100
Task decomposition? A.I. services. Talent matching? Existing marketplaces.

58
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:21,300
Crowdfunding? Existing platforms. Why stay thin? Because thin protocols are hard to capture.

59
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:25,800
There's nothing to bribe. No operational role to corrupt. No C.E.O.

60
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:30,420
to take on a yacht trip. Just code that moves money toward measured outcomes.

61
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:35,180
Your species has a habit of corrupting every institution you build.

62
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:41,620
You corrupt them the way water corrodes pipes: inevitably, given enough time and contact.

63
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:48,520
This one is boring enough that nobody bothers. The Core Innovation: Pay for Results.

64
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:54,660
Every dollar flows based on results, not promises: Patients vote with their enrollment.

65
00:06:55,360 --> 00:07:00,080
Researchers get paid for attracting patients. Outcomes determine continued funding.

66
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:08,280
Campaigns that deliver get more; failures get defunded. No one gets paid for: Writing grant proposals.

67
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:13,120
Attending review committees. Publishing papers about why their research might work someday.

68
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:20,340
This is how most other industries work. You pay contractors when they build the house, not when they promise to build it.

69
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:25,310
You pay farmers when they grow the food, not when they apply for a farming license.

70
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:31,360
Your Data Commons: Publish Everything. Your current system hides failures.

71
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:36,060
Companies bury negative results. Researchers don't publish what didn't work.

72
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:43,320
Your scientists waste billions repeating mistakes someone else already made because they literally cannot find out those

73
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:49,260
mistakes were already made. It's like your whole species has amnesia, but only for the embarrassing parts.

74
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:55,060
You remember your triumphs in high definition and forget your failures completely.

75
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:59,120
This is called "being human." It's also called "why you keep dying.".

76
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:08,380
Your Decentralized Institutes of Health, or D.I.H., requires one hundred percent open publication of all data, positive

77
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:15,120
and negative, as a condition of funding: Every trial, every result, every dataset published.

78
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:20,320
A.I. models scan the global data commons, finding patterns humans miss.

79
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:25,360
Failed experiments become shared knowledge, not repeated waste.

80
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:34,299
This is intelligence sharing in the SHAEF analogy. Your Allies won partly because they shared Ultra intercepts across commands.

81
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:40,100
Your war on disease loses because everyone guards their failures like trade secrets.

82
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:44,370
Think of it as a group chat where everyone shares what didn't work.

83
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:48,860
Except the group is your entire species and the topic is death prevention.

84
00:08:49,900 --> 00:09:00,560
Governance: Market Mechanism plus Wishocracy. Your D.I.H. uses two allocation mechanisms: Patient subsidies (market mechanism):

85
00:09:01,280 --> 00:09:07,680
Research funding follows patient choice. Patients enroll in trials they believe in; funding flows accordingly.

86
00:09:08,540 --> 00:09:15,140
Variable subsidy rates ensure rare diseases remain viable. No committees decide "cancer versus Alzheimer's.".

87
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:24,710
Wishocracy: For infrastructure and public goods only. Aggregates preferences through pairwise comparisons ("E.H.R.

88
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:30,620
integration or security audits?"). The Incentive Stack: Making R.O.I.

89
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:37,820
the Selfish Choice. Your D.I.H. doesn't rely on altruism. Altruism is lovely but flaky.

90
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:43,000
It shows up when it feels like it, cancels last minute, and is always "busy that weekend."

91
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:46,660
Instead, your D.I.H. pays everyone to do the highest-R.O.I.

92
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:54,560
thing. Greed is more dependable than kindness. Your entire economic system proves this daily.

93
00:09:54,620 --> 00:10:01,460
Nobody ever forgot to be greedy. For researchers, self-interest is money and prestige, the D.I.H.

94
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:06,820
incentive is per-patient subsidies (higher for rare diseases), and R.O.I.

95
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:15,580
alignment means attracting patients equals getting paid. For patients, self-interest is health and compensation, the D.I.H.

96
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:20,180
incentive is subsidies that scale with trial importance, and R.O.I.

97
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:24,520
alignment means joining high-priority trials equals more paid.

98
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:30,440
For politicians, self-interest is re-election and legacy, the D.I.H.

99
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:37,280
incentive is incentive alignment bonds, or I.A.B.s, tied to disease outcomes, and R.O.I.

100
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:45,860
alignment means better outcomes equal a bigger bonus. For funders, self-interest is impact and returns, the D.I.H.

101
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:50,480
incentive is quadratic matching and outcome tracking, and R.O.I.

102
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:55,180
alignment means high-R.O.I. donations result in amplified impact.

103
00:10:56,160 --> 00:11:03,940
For data providers, self-interest is revenue, the D.I.H. incentive is fees tied to data utility, and R.O.I.

104
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:11,780
alignment means more useful data equals more revenue. For campaigns, self-interest is funding, the D.I.H.

105
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:17,900
incentive is results-based continued funding, and R.O.I. alignment means higher R.O.I.

106
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:26,980
equals more funding. Every incentive is R.O.I.-weighted. Not just "do good" but "do the MOST good per dollar per hour."

107
00:11:27,700 --> 00:11:32,430
Your decentralized institutes of health, or D.I.H., makes the highest-R.O.I.

108
00:11:33,300 --> 00:11:39,920
action the selfish choice for every actor. Selfishness, properly directed, cures cancer.

109
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:46,100
How Your Researchers Get Paid. Your traditional system: Write a grant proposal.

110
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:51,140
Hope a committee likes it. Get paid to try. Maybe produce results.

111
00:11:51,380 --> 00:12:00,860
Maybe not. Get paid either way. Your D.I.H. system: Per-patient subsidies: The more patients who join your trial, the more

112
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:09,660
funding you get. Patients vote with their enrollment. Variable subsidy rates: Rare diseases get higher per-patient subsidies.

113
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:17,740
Small patient pools don't mean small budgets. Results-based continuation: Deliver results, get more funding.

114
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:24,220
Don't deliver, get defunded. Like every other job on your planet except, somehow, research.

115
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:30,640
Pay your scientists like you pay your plumbers: for fixing the problem, not for explaining why it's hard.

116
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:36,580
How It Works For Your Patients. Your D.I.H. doesn't replace your healthcare system.

117
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:41,120
It adds an "experimental treatment insurance layer" on top.

118
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:48,620
Your species already understands insurance. You just hadn't thought to apply it to not dying of untreated diseases.

119
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:54,580
Sarah has Type 2 Diabetes. Old system: Metformin not working.

120
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:59,000
No other options covered. Pays five hundred dollars per month for branded drug.

121
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:06,350
Still has poor control. With your D.I.H.: Her doctor checks the trial network during a regular visit.

122
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:10,870
Sees five relevant trials. Recommends one based on Sarah's profile.

123
00:13:11,340 --> 00:13:16,150
Sarah enrolls with one click. The trial coverage pool covers all costs.

124
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:21,120
Sarah pays a thirty dollar copay. Gets an experimental drug that might work better.

125
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,640
Reports blood sugar via app. Her data helps the next patient.

126
00:13:26,440 --> 00:13:33,260
Total time added to doctor visit: three minutes. Cost per patient per month: D.I.H.

127
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:40,340
trial coverage: three hundred ten to two thousand five hundred ten dollars (medication plus monitoring plus data collection).

128
00:13:40,980 --> 00:13:45,780
Traditional trial: six thousand eight hundred to thirteen thousand six hundred dollars.

129
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:55,100
Efficiency gain: seventy-five to eighty percent. Why your doctors will cooperate: More treatment options for desperate patients.

130
00:13:56,160 --> 00:14:00,040
Continuing medical education, or C.M.E., credits for participation.

131
00:14:00,860 --> 00:14:07,760
No liability (covered by trial insurance). One click in their electronic medical record, or E.M.R.

132
00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:15,440
No new systems to learn. Why your patients will use it: Affordable access to experimental treatments (twenty to fifty dollar

133
00:14:15,440 --> 00:14:20,200
copay). Doctor-recommended. Insurance-like coverage (familiar model).

134
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:24,300
No financial risk. They help others while helping themselves.

135
00:14:25,020 --> 00:14:28,640
It's like being a guinea pig, except the guinea pig gets paid and gets medicine.

136
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:37,740
How Your Politicians Get Aligned. Your politicians optimize for reelection, status, and post-office careers.

137
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:41,880
"Humans continuing to exist" doesn't appear on their performance review.

138
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:45,920
This is a design flaw in your democracy, not a moral failing.

139
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:53,780
Well, it's both. But mostly it's a design flaw, because if you put saints in a badly designed system, you get the same results.

140
00:14:54,300 --> 00:15:03,240
You just feel more betrayed. Incentive Alignment Bonds, or I.A.B.s, solve this by making "support pragmatic clinical trial

141
00:15:03,240 --> 00:15:09,960
funding" the career-optimal move: Public Good Scores track voting records on health policy.

142
00:15:10,020 --> 00:15:14,280
Electoral support flows to high-scorers via independent PACs.

143
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:20,980
Post-office opportunities (fellowships, boards) reserved for leaders who governed well.

144
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:28,880
No bribes. No corruption. Just a standing rule: support policies that measurably reduce suffering and your political career

145
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:33,220
gets easier. Oppose them and it gets harder. Your politicians will figure it out.

146
00:15:33,660 --> 00:15:40,700
They're not stupid. They got elected, which requires a very specific and impressive type of intelligence, none of which

147
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:45,520
involves curing diseases. How the Money Flows. The Architecture.

148
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:51,660
The one percent Treaty Fund: Holds the treasury (from the one percent Treaty).

149
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:56,180
Allocates between infrastructure and public goods via Wishocracy.

150
00:15:56,820 --> 00:16:02,900
Funds campaigns, not bureaucracies. No C.E.O., no board, no one to corrupt.

151
00:16:04,060 --> 00:16:10,700
Your Decentralized Institute of Health, or D.I.H.: A thin coordination protocol.

152
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:17,900
Receives funding from the one percent Treaty Fund. Research allocation via patient subsidies (market mechanism).

153
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:27,520
Verifies results, pays for outcomes. Your D.F.D.A.: A funded campaign, not part of your D.I.H.

154
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:33,360
itself. Provides technical infrastructure for trials. Competes with other research models for funding.

155
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:38,660
Has no budget authority (just a service provider). The Fund Flow.

156
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:47,220
Your one percent Treaty redirects twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year from global military budgets into the one

157
00:16:47,220 --> 00:16:54,780
percent Treaty Fund. But not all of it reaches research (some of it goes to keeping the machine running): VICTORY Bond investors

158
00:16:54,780 --> 00:17:01,320
receive ten percent, totaling two point seven two billion dollars, to repay campaign funders.

159
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:09,440
Political incentives, or I.A.B.s, receive ten percent, totaling two point seven two billion dollars, to keep politicians

160
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:18,140
aligned. The Research treasury receives eighty percent, totaling twenty-one point eight billion dollars, for patient subsidies

161
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:23,940
(market allocation). What Gets Funded: Market Failures Only.

162
00:17:24,099 --> 00:17:29,820
Most research allocation happens automatically (patients choose trials, funding flows there).

163
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:34,150
Your one percent Treaty Fund primarily funds market failures.

164
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:37,540
These are the things your ecosystem can't handle on its own.

165
00:17:38,260 --> 00:17:43,820
Infrastructure Development and operations. Competing alternative implementations.

166
00:17:44,640 --> 00:17:50,920
Data commons infrastructure (storage, processing). Security audits and fraud detection systems.

167
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:57,420
True Public Goods (No Revenue Model) Patient trial participation subsidies.

168
00:17:57,740 --> 00:18:03,240
Negative results publishing. Replication studies. This is minimal by design.

169
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:11,260
Your ecosystem handles most research funding automatically: companies register treatments, patients join trials, revenue

170
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:20,300
flows, research happens. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health, or D.I.H., only directs the one percent Treaty Fund to

171
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:26,200
cover what the market genuinely can't. What Your D.I.H. Outsources (and Why).

172
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:34,680
Your D.I.H. is intentionally minimal. It outsources everything operational because the best way to avoid corruption is to

173
00:18:34,680 --> 00:18:41,920
have nothing worth corrupting: Crowdfunding is outsourced to Gitcoin, Juicebox, et cetera, because they already exist and

174
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:48,780
work. Task decomposition is outsourced to A.I. services because machines are better at this.

175
00:18:48,820 --> 00:18:54,190
Talent matching is outsourced to existing marketplaces so you don't reinvent LinkedIn.

176
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:59,480
Trial infrastructure is outsourced to the decentralized F.D.A., or D.F.D.A.

177
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:06,960
(a funded campaign), to separate concerns. Data storage is outsourced to competing providers for market competition.

178
00:19:08,080 --> 00:19:14,200
How Campaigns Plug In. Your D.F.D.A. is the main example of a funded campaign.

179
00:19:14,600 --> 00:19:18,900
It's not part of your D.I.H. It's a service provider competing for funding.

180
00:19:19,380 --> 00:19:24,560
If it stops performing, you fund something better. This is how your species runs restaurants.

181
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:33,960
Time to try it with medicine. Campaign Lifecycle. 1. Proposal: Submit campaign description, budget, milestones.

182
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:39,720
2. Wishocracy vote: Humanity decides priority relative to alternatives.

183
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:46,660
3. Funding: Treasury allocates based on vote plus Optimocracy recommendations.

184
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:52,260
4. Execution: Campaign delivers services (trials, infrastructure, et cetera).

185
00:19:53,220 --> 00:20:01,700
5. Verification: Measure outcomes against milestones. 6. Continuation: Results determine next year's funding.

186
00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:07,600
Anti-Capture Design. Your current system is trivially captured.

187
00:20:08,160 --> 00:20:12,700
Concentrate billions of dollars in a few committees, and lobbyists will find them.

188
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:20,000
This is as predictable as gravity, and your species keeps being surprised by it the way a dog is surprised by a vacuum cleaner.

189
00:20:21,120 --> 00:20:27,720
Every single time. Your D.I.H. makes capture economically irrational.

190
00:20:27,720 --> 00:20:36,540
How Your D.I.H. Resists Capture. The mechanism No C.E.O. means there is nothing to bribe, and the capture cost is not applicable.

191
00:20:37,360 --> 00:20:42,500
Algorithmic governance uses rules in smart contracts; you can't bribe an if-statement.

192
00:20:43,760 --> 00:20:48,060
A public ledger ensures every dollar is tracked and corruption is visible.

193
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:54,380
The protocol is forkable, meaning anyone can clone it; capture triggers replacement.

194
00:20:55,400 --> 00:21:01,040
Distributed voting involves millions voting via Wishocracy, so lobbying doesn't scale.

195
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:08,860
Outcome-based funding ensures results determine allocation, making gaming harder than performing.

196
00:21:09,140 --> 00:21:17,400
